title: Losing to the fuse
story: fernhill
continues: folly
description: The other branch out of the same folly doorway — the bare `cut the
  fuse` that does nothing, and the three turns that end the story.

---

# The trap that makes this branch reachable at all: the player IS holding the
# garden shears (see fuse-cut.transcript, same starting state), and the bare
# command still refuses. The instrument has to be named.
> cut the fuse
[OK: contains "You need something to cut the fuse with"]
[OK: not contains "The shears bite through the waxed cord"]
[STATE: true, player.inventory contains shears]

# `on every turn while it is lit` — the cycling hiss is the clock the player
# can hear.
> wait
[OK: contains "The fuse hisses on, eating its slow way toward the fireworks"]

# `3 turns later / kill the player fuse-blast when the fuse is lit`.
> wait
[OK: contains "The cache goes up in one white roar, and the folly keeps its secrets after all"]

# ORDERING BUG, asserted as it behaves: the fuse's per-turn phrase still fires
# on the turn that kills the player, so a spark line prints AFTER the blast
# that ended the story.
[OK: contains "Sparks walk the waxed cord, closer now"]

# The transcript has to STOP here. Any further command — even `score` — comes
# back as `Error: Engine is not running`, and the runner classifies that as a
# command error rather than as output, so no assertion can catch it:
#
#     > look                                             FAIL
#       Error: Engine is not running
#
# A losing branch is therefore only expressible as a file whose last command is
# the one that ends the story. There is no way to assert on what a player sees
# after an ending, and nothing says so until the run goes red.
